{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Stroke is the most common cause of complex disability. 1 Neuropsychiatric symptoms are often associated with stroke; among them, depression is the most prevalent. Prevalence of major depression in people after stroke is 25% at the acute stage and 31% at 3 months poststroke 2 ; however, few individuals receive effective management for depressive symptoms post-stroke. 3 Unaddressed depressive symptoms can lead to devastating effects; not only do they stunt progress in therapy, but they can also negatively affect stroke outcomes, with increased morbidity, mortality, and poorer functional recovery. 4 Previous studies have been conducted to identify appropriate interventions for post-stroke depression; two common types are pharmacological interventions and non-pharmacological interventions (NPIs). 5-9 Three meta-analyses have been conducted to provide synthesized evidence on the effectiveness of pharmacological interventions for depressive symptoms following stroke, but results were mixed. 5,6,9 Chen et al. demonstrated the efficacy of antidepressants in reducing depressive symptoms in this population. 5 Hackett et al. also provided evidence for antidepressants but reported only small effects on the amelioration of depressive symptoms. 6 Conversely, a recent review performed a network meta-analysis of nine types of antidepressants to determine their efficacy, acceptability, and tolerability. Although three antidepressants, doxepin, paroxetine, and nortriptyline, were more effective than a placebo, the review noted that the extent to which symptom reduction of these antidepressants was clinically meaningful is uncertain because diverse results were found in their acceptability and tolerability. 9 With mixed conclusions, the effectiveness of pharmacological interventions for post-stroke depressive symptoms has not yet been determined.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes current knowledge (prevalence, existence of pharmacological and non-pharmacological interventions) and highlights uncertainties and mixed conclusions regarding effectiveness of pharmacological interventions."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Moreover, it is recognized that the application of pharmacological interventions might be further limited due to adverse effects, long response times, potential medication adverse events, and low adherence rates. 8,10,11 For these reasons, an alternative, or addition, to pharmaceutical options is essential to ensure effective management of depressive symptoms after stroke. NPIs, which are defined as science-based and non-invasive interventions on human health, may provide an alternative to ameliorate depressive symptoms. Several NPIs for depression exist, such as complementary and alternative therapy (CAT), exercise, psychosocial therapy, non-invasive cranial stimulation, and multifactorial behavioral treatment. 12 Currently, an abundance of evidence supports the effectiveness of NPIs for depression in other clinical populations. 10,13 ... However, none of these reviews quantitatively demonstrated the effectiveness of different types of NPIs. Therefore, to address this research gap, this study aimed to conduct a meta-analysis to synthesize and compare the available evidence regarding the effectiveness of various NPIs for treating depression in people following stroke.",
      "reason": "The authors explain limitations of pharmacological treatments, posit NPIs as needed alternatives, identify a gap (no quantitative syntheses of NPIs), and state the aim to conduct a meta-analysis, justifying the review's importance."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Three reviews have identified several NPIs for post-stroke depression and have qualitatively provided evidence. 8,11,14 In particular, the latest review indicated that therapies demonstrating a reduction in depressive symptoms include problem-solving therapy, acupuncture, repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation, music therapy, exercise, light therapy, motivational interviewing, and robotic-assisted therapy. 8 However, none of these reviews quantitatively demonstrated the effectiveness of different types of NPIs.",
      "reason": "The manuscript cites prior reviews and explains why the current review is needed (prior reviews were qualitative and did not quantitatively compare effectiveness), justifying an updated/novel meta-analysis."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No retrieved evidence describes a proposed mechanism of action for the non-pharmacological interventions; the introduction defines NPIs as 'science-based and non-invasive' but does not explain their mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No retrieved evidence mentions or presents a logic model showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes for complex or multifactorial interventions."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "90"
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2; text_6",
      "quote": "Objective: To compare the effectiveness of non-pharmacological interventions on depressive symptoms in people after stroke.\n\nThe PICOS (Participants, Interventions, Comparisons, Outcomes, Study Designs) checklist was used to identify the inclusion criteria in this review. 17 Participants (P): This review included trials if they targeted persons with stroke (ischemic and hemorrhagic), regardless of comorbidities and any previous treatments, but excluded those with manic or psychotic episodes/symptoms in order to reduce biases associated with non-target symptoms. Participants were included whether or not they received pharmacological interventions (any dosage or duration) for depression or other illnesses.",
      "reason": "The abstract provides an explicit objective statement about comparing effectiveness of non-pharmacological interventions for depressive symptoms after stroke, and the Methods indicates use of the PICOS framework to define the review scope (Participants, Interventions, Comparisons, Outcomes, Study designs), satisfying the requirement to state objectives/questions with a relevant question-formulation framework."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "The PICOS (Participants, Interventions, Comparisons, Outcomes, Study Designs) checklist was used to identify the inclusion criteria in this review. 17 ... Interventions (I) and controls (C): This review considered trials that compared the effects of NPIs for post-stroke individuals with depressive symptoms (eg, music therapy, massage therapy, behavioral therapy, or physical exercise with any frequency, intensity, or duration) to control conditions, such as usual treatment.",
      "reason": "For this review of intervention effects, the planned comparisons are explicitly stated using the PICOS framework: interventions (non-pharmacological interventions) compared to control conditions (such as usual treatment)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "The PICOS (Participants, Interventions, Comparisons, Outcomes, Study Designs) checklist was used to identify the inclusion criteria in this review. 17 Participants (P): This review included trials if they targeted persons with stroke (ischemic and hemorrhagic), regardless of comorbidities and any previous treatments, but excluded those with manic or psychotic episodes/symptoms in order to reduce biases associated with non-target symptoms. Participants were included whether or not they received pharmacological interventions (any dosage or duration) for depression or other illnesses. Interventions (I) and controls (C): This review considered trials that compared the effects of NPIs for post-stroke individuals with depressive symptoms (eg, music therapy, massage therapy, behavioral therapy, or physical exercise with any frequency, intensity, or duration) to control conditions, such as usual treatment. Outcomes (O): The primary outcome of this review was depressive symptoms, measured by any validated depression scale. Study designs (S): This review considered any RCT with a pre-post design, meaning that outcomes were measured at pre-intervention, post-intervention, and follow-up.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly specifies PICOS components: participant eligibility (stroke types, exclusions), eligible interventions and controls, outcome (depressive symptoms measured by validated scales), and eligible study design (RCTs with pre-post measures)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Trials published between January 2000 and August 2018 were considered for inclusion; this date range reflects findings from an initial search in MEDLINE and CINAHL, which showed no relevant trials before 2000.",
      "reason": "The review states report-characteristic restrictions (publication date range). Additional evidence (text_6) notes restriction to randomized controlled trials written in English and published in peer-reviewed journals, specifying language and publication status."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_12",
      "quote": "Of 22 included trials, we excluded three trials indicating publication bias and six trials reporting insufficient data. Reasons for insufficient data were: (1) authors of three trials did not respond to emails for the requested information, 28-30 (2) authors of one trial refused to provide data, 31 (3) authors from one trial did not keep the data, 26 and (4) authors of one trial did not have the requested information. Thus, 13 trials were included in the final metaanalysis.",
      "reason": "The manuscript documents exclusions due to unavailable or unreported outcome data and gives reasons (e.g., authors did not respond), i.e., exclusion because results were not reported. However, it does not explicitly distinguish these from exclusions because outcomes were not measured in the study, so the distinction is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_12",
      "quote": "These trials were grouped into four types: CAT (n = 5), exercise (n = 4), psychosocial therapy (n = 2), and multifactorial therapy (n = 2) (Table 2). ... These 12 trials consisted of four types of NPI: (1) CAT (five trials, 228 participants), (2) exercise (four trials, 263 participants), (3) psychosocial therapy (two trials, 216 participants), and (4) multifactorial therapy (one trial, 186 participants). Overall results, combining the outcomes of all included NPIs, showed a significant effect size on depressive symptoms post-intervention ... In particular, beneficial effects favoring CAT ... and psychosocial therapy ... were observed",
      "reason": "The review specifies the groups used in the synthesis (types of NPIs) and reports meta-analytic comparisons of these groups versus control, linking groupings to the review's comparisons/objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Trials published between January 2000 and August 2018 were considered for inclusion; this date range reflects findings from an initial search in MEDLINE and CINAHL, which showed no relevant trials before 2000.",
      "reason": "A rationale is provided for the date restriction. Additionally, text_7 explains rationale for methodological quality restrictions: \"The five mandatory questions were chosen to avoid potential detection bias (Q5, Q8, Q9), decrease selection bias (Q6), and ensure the use of appropriate statistical analysis (Q10).\""
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

